  "theory": {
    "intro": "For a **composite logarithm** $y=\\ln f(x)$ — a logarithm with a function inside — the chain rule gives $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{f'(x)}{f(x)}$. Differentiate the inside function and put it over the inside function, unchanged.",
    "facts": [
      "Rule: $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\ln f(x)=\\dfrac{f'(x)}{f(x)}$, valid wherever $f(x)>0$.",
      "The derivative of the inside goes on top; the inside itself stays on the bottom, unchanged.",
      "Linear inside: $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\ln(ax+b)=\\dfrac{a}{ax+b}$.",
      "Common error: the answer is not $\\dfrac{1}{f(x)}$, which drops $f'(x)$, and not $\\dfrac{1}{f'(x)}$.",
      "Simplify first where a log law allows it, since $\\ln\\left(x^{3}\\right)=3\\ln x$ and $\\ln\\dfrac{x}{x+1}=\\ln x-\\ln(x+1)$.",
      "A constant factor inside disappears: $\\ln(7x)=\\ln 7+\\ln x$, so $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\ln(7x)=\\dfrac{1}{x}$.",
      "When the logarithm sits in a product or quotient, use that rule first and $\\dfrac{f'(x)}{f(x)}$ only for the logarithm factor.",
      "Domain: $\\ln f(x)$ exists only where $f(x)>0$, so check any value of $x$ you substitute."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Check whether a log law simplifies the logarithm before differentiating.",
      "Identify the inside function $f(x)$ and differentiate it to get $f'(x)$.",
      "Write the derivative as $\\dfrac{f'(x)}{f(x)}$.",
      "Apply the product, quotient or chain rule if the logarithm sits inside a larger expression, then simplify."
    ]
  },
